WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:06.394
A bank statement is a list of lines, and
reading one is easy to get slightly wrong.

2
00:00:06.694 --> 00:00:11.074
Miss one line, or read one number
wrong, and the ledger is off for good.

3
00:00:11.374 --> 00:00:12.724
Nobody notices.

4
00:00:13.224 --> 00:00:17.187
So Summa doesn't trust what it reads
until the statement proves it.

5
00:00:17.587 --> 00:00:21.467
Every statement prints an opening
balance and a closing balance.

6
00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:25.853
Summa starts from the opening
balance and adds every line it read.

7
00:00:26.153 --> 00:00:31.027
If the total lands exactly on the closing
balance, the statement is trusted.

8
00:00:31.427 --> 00:00:35.447
If it's off by even one unit,
the whole statement is rejected.

9
00:00:35.947 --> 00:00:37.200
There's a second trap.

10
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.328
Two exports from the same
account can overlap,

11
00:00:40.328 --> 00:00:43.340
and the first one can stop
halfway through a day.

12
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:48.071
Cut by date, and you either count that
day twice or lose the rest of it.

13
00:00:48.471 --> 00:00:52.926
So Summa finds the line where the first
export's running balance stopped,

14
00:00:52.926 --> 00:00:54.391
and cuts right after it.

15
00:00:54.791 --> 00:00:57.826
Nothing is counted twice,
and nothing is lost.

16
00:00:58.226 --> 00:01:02.456
Every statement line in the ledger
came from a statement that added up.
